00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.540
And then next to that, we
have the customer-supplied

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.290
encryption keys if you
really want to have

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.140
more control over your keys.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:04.950
And in that feature, we actually
don't keep your keys at all.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:08.420
So when you actually put
data up in your GCS bucket,

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:12.170
for example, then you need
to provide a secret that we

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:13.800
use to help encrypt the data.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.040
And then every time you
want to access that data,

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:17.620
you have to re-provide it.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:19.579
And we use that secret
to help decrypt the data

00:04:19.579 --> 00:04:20.480
and then we get rid of it.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:21.836
It never gets stored anywhere.

00:04:21.836 --> 00:04:23.210
It definitely
doesn't go to disk.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:24.540
It's only in memory.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:29.330
So it's something that's
available for GCS and GCE,

00:04:29.330 --> 00:04:31.340
but not for other services.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.530
One of the main
problems is that when

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:36.630
we say we don't keep the key,
we really don't keep the key.

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:40.040
And so if you lose a key,
there's absolutely nothing

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:41.120
we can do about it.

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:44.750
And if you don't
provide the key,

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:46.800
there's really nothing we
can do about your data.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.750
So for services that require
us to do background operations,

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:53.650
such as like impaction,
for example, which

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:56.000
