00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:32.500
Wow, you guys are awesome.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.560
Yeah, so they give
you the insurances

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.350
of what an HSM provides.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:38.375
So we can't get
the key material.

00:06:38.375 --> 00:06:39.750
I mean, all the
things like that.

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:41.750
Tightly bound to the hardware.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:47.040
And it basically gives you
more options now for your KMS.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.370
So let's start off with--

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.550
it sounds like most of you guys
know about this, but just kind

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.174
of indulge me and
I'll just try to make

00:06:52.174 --> 00:06:56.500
sure everyone's kind of a level
set and explain what an HSM is.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:59.670
So an HSM is a Hardware
Security Module.

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:01.290
So it basically is
a physical device

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:05.970
for managing keys and
performing crypto operations.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.220
It does a little bit more
that, but essentially that's

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:09.735
what it's really used for.

00:07:09.735 --> 00:07:13.530
And people like these things,
largely because they can create

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:15.000
keys that are non-exportable.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.250
The hardware will
provide some guarantees

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:19.650
that once you create the
key inside this thing,

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.500
it's not exportable, kind of.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.320
I mean, the vendors like to
allow the keys to move around

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:26.640
between their own devices.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:30.510
But you can't export
anything else.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:34.980
And then they provide tamper
evidence and tamper resistance.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:39.032
So if you try to break
into one of these things,

00:07:39.032 --> 00:07:41.490
then they're saying that they'll
probably be able to notice

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:42.630
and they'll record it.

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:45.330
And if you actually try to
compromise and you get close,

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:47.740
then it'll do things
like exert all the keys

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:50.850
so the keys are completely
wiped from the system.

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:52.710
And the other thing
is that they come

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:55.545
with FIPS 140-2 certification.

00:07:55.545 --> 00:07:56.776
And-- yeah, please.

00:07:56.776 --> 00:07:58.900
AUDIENCE: Why not three,
if it's a hardware device?

00:07:58.900 --> 00:07:59.340
IL-SUNG LEE: Sorry?

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.298
AUDIENCE: You haven't
achieved FIPS level 3 yet

00:08:01.298 --> 00:08:04.280
if you're doing penetration?

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:06.040
IL-SUNG LEE: Well,
the FIPS level 3--

00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:08.840
FIPS 140 dash level 3
dictates some other conditions

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:10.910
around what you have
to provide and such.

00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:13.640
HSMs typically provide
level 2 or level 3.

00:08:13.640 --> 00:08:14.140
Right?

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:14.510
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:15.450
IL-SUNG LEE: Yeah.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:16.970
And I'll talk a little bit
more about this, as well,

00:08:16.970 --> 00:08:18.050
for our service.

00:08:18.050 --> 00:08:20.950
But this certification
is important.

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:22.700
It's a certification
that comes up

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:26.040
by NIST in the US government.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:29.180
It's the only real good
certification that's

00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:30.540
available for crypto modules.

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:31.700
And that's what it defines.

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:33.283
So as a result, a
lot of people depend

00:08:33.283 --> 00:08:35.425
upon using FIPS as
a kind of a gauge

00:08:35.425 --> 00:08:38.280
to whether this is adequately
implemented or not.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:41.789
And so again, like you
asked, for level 1,

00:08:41.789 --> 00:08:43.039
you can do this with software.

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:44.747
Typically for anything
greater than that,

00:08:44.747 --> 00:08:46.760
you need a hardware
module, like an HSM.

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:49.460
Now, in terms of main
use cases, lot of people

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:52.100
use this for
regulatory compliance.

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:54.590
So if you're doing
things like you're

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:57.930
in the financial industry,
maybe the health care,

00:08:57.930 --> 00:09:00.560
you do some payments,
things like that, you likely

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:02.360
do require to have
something that

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:04.970
actually has some high bar
around protection of keys.

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:07.760
Maybe you have specific
NIST FIPS requirements.

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:11.120
Then these HSMs become
extremely valuable.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:14.720
Another thing is is that
whether it's right or wrong,

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:19.430
people feel more safe with
putting their keys in hardware

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:22.250
devices because of some of the
properties I listed before.

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:24.980
And so if you have
high-value keys, for example,

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:28.610
like perhaps the keys for
your top signing cert,

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:30.380
then you may want to
put it into an HSM,

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:32.546
just to make sure that you
have that extra assurance

00:09:32.546 --> 00:09:34.280
that they're protected.

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:35.390
Make sense?

00:09:35.390 --> 00:09:37.720
You guys now know as
much as I do about HSMs.

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:41.480
So that's great.

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:43.360
Now, what's Cloud HSM?

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:45.920
So Cloud HSM is
basically taking the HSMs

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:48.800
and making it into
managed service.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:51.410
And when I say managed,
I'd like to kind of clarify

00:09:51.410 --> 00:09:53.480
what Google's definition
of managed is.

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:56.317
So managed means that basically
we do everything for you.

00:09:56.317 --> 00:09:57.900
The only thing you
have to worry about

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:03.380
is the API for doing the
logical, application-level API

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:04.490
interaction.

00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:06.305
So when we manage
it, it means that you

00:10:06.305 --> 00:10:08.180
don't have to worry
about any of the hardware

00:10:08.180 --> 00:10:11.570
or the infrastructure or
anything else like that at all.

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:13.790
We'll make sure that the
patching of the hardware

00:10:13.790 --> 00:10:14.990
is all done by us.

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:17.190
If there's any kind of
availability concerns,

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:19.040
we've fully
clusterized everything.

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.010
We have a very smart layer
that actually makes sure

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:24.350
that it's completely available.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:26.930
If there's scaling
issues, we autoscale.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:29.115
We just basically restart--

00:10:29.115 --> 00:10:31.220
that was a really
interesting story.

00:10:31.220 --> 00:10:34.160
Yeah, we basically are able to
scale pretty much horizontally

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:36.380
with any kind of workload
that you bring along.

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:38.060
So there's no
concerns around that.

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:40.760
And they're also rooted
in physical HSMs.

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:43.684
So the key material is not
available outside of HSMs,

00:10:43.684 --> 00:10:46.100
which means that you can't get
access to the key material,

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:48.470
but neither can we.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:50.270
And if you take a
look at that diagram

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:52.190
there, the way that
you think about this

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:55.100
is that the Cloud
HSM feature actually

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:59.589
leverages the exact same
API frontend as Cloud KMS.

00:10:59.589 --> 00:11:01.130
So if you're familiar
with Cloud KMS,

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:03.180
and I'll show you
in a demo later on,

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:05.180
it's actually as
simple as just flipping

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:07.100
a flag when you create the key.

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:08.600
And all of a sudden,
everything else

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:10.016
that you have in
terms of the APIs

00:11:10.016 --> 00:11:13.970
you use, the client
libraries, this works,

00:11:13.970 --> 00:11:16.190
except that the keys are in HSM.

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:19.186
And I'll also explain to you and
in a demo about how we actually

00:11:19.186 --> 00:11:20.810
prove to you that
the keys are actually

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:23.604
in the physical device.

00:11:23.604 --> 00:11:25.020
So in the diagram
here, going back

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:27.330
to that, when a request
comes in from a client,

00:11:27.330 --> 00:11:29.160
they go to the KMS service.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:32.880
And the KMS service knows that
this key is a software key.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:35.430
So we go down to the
software key backend.

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.030
We do the operations there.

00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:39.030
Or if it's a hardware
key, then all the requests

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:41.310
get routed to the
hardware layer,

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:44.096
which actually goes to the
individual hardware node.

00:11:44.096 --> 00:11:45.720
We obviously use some
smart logic there

00:11:45.720 --> 00:11:49.560
in terms of picking
the right node.

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:51.826
And in that case, all
the crypt operations

00:11:51.826 --> 00:11:53.700
and all the key creations
and everything else

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:55.825
actually happened
right in the hardware.

00:11:55.825 --> 00:11:57.450
It's not that we're
doing the software.

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:59.670
These things actually
happen in the software--

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:00.545
I mean, the hardware.

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:05.510
Now, why should
you use Cloud HSM?

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:07.691
Again, I'll say this a
lot because this is what

00:12:07.691 --> 00:12:08.940
a lot of customers care about.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:13.230
But compliance comes up
very, very, very often.

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:15.150
So if you have a
compliance need,

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:18.510
whether you want to or not,
it's going to come up again.

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:21.360
Now, regionalization is another
really, really cool feature

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:24.340
that we provide as part
of the new product.

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:29.220
So if you create a
key within Cloud HSM,

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:31.740
then that key is actually
cryptographically bound

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:34.530
to the hardware devices
within that region.

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:38.040
So suppose I go and create
a key in US Central 1.

00:12:38.040 --> 00:12:40.140
Those hardware devices
in US Central 1

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:43.770
are the only ones that are
able to use our crypto key.

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:47.070
And so the way that we have
things architected right now

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:48.510
is that the keys
are always stored

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:51.570
in one of our highly
available [INAUDIBLE] systems.

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:54.040
And those things can actually
move the keys around.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:56.460
And in fact, we don't
provide any guarantees

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:00.210
around the location
of those stored keys.

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:04.450
But the keys themselves
are protected by the HSM,

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:06.120
or at least HSM keys.

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.330
So they can only
be used, unwrapped

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:10.190
and used by those HSMs.

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:12.090
HSMs in a different
region cannot use them.

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:18.660
Right now the Cloud HSM
feature is currently in alpha,

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:20.130
soon to go into beta.

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:22.170
And right now we're
located in US Central 1.

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:25.350
But we will be releasing the
beta across the United States

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:27.235
and then slowly start
rolling out globally.

00:13:27.235 --> 00:13:28.860
So this is something
that you should be

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:30.068
able to see around the world.

00:13:30.068 --> 00:13:32.558
Yes, please?

00:13:32.558 --> 00:13:36.735
AUDIENCE: You mentioned that
the Cloud HSM keys are regional.

00:13:36.735 --> 00:13:37.860
What about the regular KMS?

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.752
Are those also region-specific?

00:13:40.752 --> 00:13:42.210
Or are they stored in the cloud?

00:13:42.210 --> 00:13:43.020
IL-SUNG LEE: The
question is whether

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:44.900
the same regionalization
guarantees

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:47.640
happen with KMS keys, KMS keys.

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:49.320
The answer is no.

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:51.930
So the Cloud KMS keys, when
you actually create the keys

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:54.150
in a certain region, they
will be very likely served

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:55.320
from that region.

00:13:55.320 --> 00:14:00.260
But the system, in order to
provide availability or to--

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:02.700
suppose that a certain
region goes down.

00:14:02.700 --> 00:14:04.890
It is able to serve that
key from different regions,

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:06.474
if necessary.

00:14:06.474 --> 00:14:07.400
AUDIENCE: Can I ask--

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:08.660
IL-SUNG LEE: Yes, please.

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:09.910
AUDIENCE: So that means that--

00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:13.790
I think you mentioned it
but I want to clarify,

00:14:13.790 --> 00:14:18.155
is that with Cloud HSM you
will not get high availability

00:14:18.155 --> 00:14:19.130
across regions?

00:14:19.130 --> 00:14:20.546
IL-SUNG LEE: Yeah,
so the question

00:14:20.546 --> 00:14:22.170
is does that mean
that with Cloud HSM,

00:14:22.170 --> 00:14:24.080
we don't get availability
across regions?

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:25.640
That's actually my next bullet.

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:28.295
So right now,
we're in one region

00:14:28.295 --> 00:14:29.420
because we're in the alpha.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:31.378
And then beta, we'll come
up with more regions.

00:14:31.378 --> 00:14:35.000
But we will actually provide
support for multi regions.

00:14:35.000 --> 00:14:38.336
So this means that, just
like Cloud KMS right now,

00:14:38.336 --> 00:14:39.710
we'll have a global
region and we

00:14:39.710 --> 00:14:43.376
will have a US region, Europe
region, and Asia region.

00:14:43.376 --> 00:14:44.750
And what these
multi regions mean

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:47.660
is that when you create a
key within, for example,

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:50.330
the US region, then
that key can actually

00:14:50.330 --> 00:14:54.490
be served out of any of the HSMs
within those multiple regions.

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:55.240
So it's up to you.

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:57.560
You can actually choose
how granular you want this.

00:14:57.560 --> 00:14:58.704
Over there, please.

00:14:58.704 --> 00:15:03.694
AUDIENCE: Could you help
explain the context of alpha?

00:15:03.694 --> 00:15:04.610
IL-SUNG LEE: Oh, sure.

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:06.130
I'm sorry.

00:15:06.130 --> 00:15:08.790
So the question is, can we
explain the context of alpha?

00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:11.610
So alpha for us is
generally, but not always,

00:15:11.610 --> 00:15:13.267
a closed release.

00:15:13.267 --> 00:15:15.600
So we have a subset of people
who are actually using it.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:18.600
They're testing it, providing
some close interactive

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:19.590
feedback.

00:15:19.590 --> 00:15:22.050
And then when we
get to beta, beta

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:25.140
is the point at which we
actually release it publicly

00:15:25.140 --> 00:15:28.540
where we will provide full
support for the product.

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:32.675
We'll provide-- you can actually
put production workloads

00:15:32.675 --> 00:15:33.550
and everything on it.

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:35.160
The one thing that
we don't necessary

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:39.223
provide until we go generally
available is the SLA.

00:15:39.223 --> 00:15:40.205
Yes?

00:15:40.205 --> 00:15:51.374
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:15:51.374 --> 00:15:53.540
IL-SUNG LEE: The question
is whether anything that--

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:55.373
the features that we're
mentioning right now

00:15:55.373 --> 00:15:57.510
would be cut from the beta?

00:15:57.510 --> 00:15:58.464
No, no.

00:15:58.464 --> 00:15:59.130
The answer's no.

00:15:59.130 --> 00:16:00.960
Everything that I'm
talking about here

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:04.260
is definitely stuff that we
will be providing in the beta.

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:05.047
Question?

00:16:05.047 --> 00:16:09.390
AUDIENCE: When will HSM
be globally available?

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:10.140
IL-SUNG LEE: Yeah.

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:12.509
Question is when will HSMs
be globally available?

00:16:12.509 --> 00:16:14.550
I can't answer that right
now, because it depends

00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:17.730
upon schedule around when we
can actually start deploying

00:16:17.730 --> 00:16:19.290
the hardware to other things.

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:22.260
The cloud HSM has been a
very interesting project,

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:24.910
at least for my career,
because it's not only software,

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:27.240
but there is a strong
hardware component to it.

00:16:27.240 --> 00:16:28.950
And so especially
since we're dealing

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:30.490
with cryptographic
devices, as well,

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:32.490
there's some
increased sensitivity.

00:16:32.490 --> 00:16:34.770
So what we will do is
that we will actually

00:16:34.770 --> 00:16:36.750
start rolling these
things out depending

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:39.570
upon what our hardware operation
schedule and everything else

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:41.040
is.

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:42.570
And we'll have to
figure this out.

00:16:42.570 --> 00:16:45.650
But I don't have that
answer right now.

00:16:45.650 --> 00:16:46.880
Yes?

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:49.806
AUDIENCE: Is the Cloud HSM
a partition or a shared

00:16:49.806 --> 00:16:50.700
partition?

00:16:50.700 --> 00:16:52.200
IL-SUNG LEE: That's
a good question.

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:54.930
So the question is whether
the Cloud HSM is a partition

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:56.269
or a shared partition.

00:16:56.269 --> 00:16:57.810
We don't follow the
traditional model

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:01.470
that you may be used to
from using a regular HSM.

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:04.400
We're not providing you
with a dedicated partition.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:08.710
Basically there's-- it's
a multi-tenant service.

00:17:08.710 --> 00:17:12.210
So the only tenant for
these HSMs is Google.

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:14.160
And we will manage the
separation of your keys

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:15.960
and everything else for you.

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:20.190
And this is a little bit of
a difference from the way

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:23.670
that things happen from your
original, traditional model.

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:25.270
But what this allows
us to do is it

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:27.960
allows us to develop an
extremely flexible and scalable

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:30.450
type of service that
allows us to provide

00:17:30.450 --> 00:17:31.920
this fully managed
type of features

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:33.390
that we hope that you'll enjoy.

00:17:36.596 --> 00:17:37.970
All right?

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:38.800
Next slide.

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:42.910
So now I want to talk a little
bit about the key features.

00:17:42.910 --> 00:17:47.177
So three things I want to
point out specifically.

00:17:47.177 --> 00:17:49.260
I want to talk a little
bit about asymmetric keys.

00:17:49.260 --> 00:17:50.520
And I think pretty
much everyone here

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:52.030
should understand what that is.

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:53.880
Statement attestation,
right this

00:17:53.880 --> 00:17:57.900
is basically the validation
that you have a key in the HSM.

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:00.240
And then CMEK, or Customer
Managed Encryption Key

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:01.210
Integration.

00:18:01.210 --> 00:18:04.690
OK, let me talk about each
one of these individually.

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:08.290
So asymmetric keys,
up till now KMS

00:18:08.290 --> 00:18:10.830
has only provided support
for symmetric keys.

00:18:10.830 --> 00:18:12.350
So you had one choice.

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:16.940
You can do AES 256 to encrypt
data, do envelope encryption,

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:19.340
or do encryption
with other services.

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:21.445
But that doesn't cover all
use cases, necessarily.

00:18:21.445 --> 00:18:23.840
And so now we're going to
allow you to actually create

00:18:23.840 --> 00:18:25.910
different types
of asymmetric keys

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:27.552
and support things
like code signing.

00:18:27.552 --> 00:18:28.760
This comes up every so often.

00:18:28.760 --> 00:18:30.490
People want to
sign certain things

00:18:30.490 --> 00:18:32.281
and they want to make
sure that the signing

00:18:32.281 --> 00:18:34.530
keys reside in the HSM itself.

00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:35.660
It's very, very typical.

00:18:35.660 --> 00:18:37.430
And it makes a lot of sense.

00:18:37.430 --> 00:18:39.110
And certificate
authorities, as well.

00:18:39.110 --> 00:18:41.120
I mean, if you work
for an enterprise,

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:43.670
then chances are
that, for example, you

00:18:43.670 --> 00:18:45.925
have internal,
non-authoritative CAs.

00:18:45.925 --> 00:18:47.300
And they're probably
doing things

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:49.599
like dishing out X.509
certificates to all

00:18:49.599 --> 00:18:51.140
your laptops and
everything else that

00:18:51.140 --> 00:18:53.154
happens in your enterprise.

00:18:53.154 --> 00:18:55.070
And so that's something
where you can actually

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:57.230
take those valuable
keys, all the root keys,

00:18:57.230 --> 00:19:00.050
and stick them
into our cloud HSM.

00:19:00.050 --> 00:19:02.990
Now we are providing
different types of keys

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:05.942
and key sizes for
signature verification.

00:19:05.942 --> 00:19:07.400
So as you can see
there, we provide

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:11.630
RSA 2k, 3k, and 4k keys.

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:15.770
And in addition, we've support
a couple of elliptic curve

00:19:15.770 --> 00:19:21.500
with two different types of
NIST curves, so P256 and P3A4.

00:19:21.500 --> 00:19:23.810
Now, for decryption
purposes, you

00:19:23.810 --> 00:19:26.312
can actually use RSA
keys for decryption.

00:19:26.312 --> 00:19:27.770
So if you choose
to actually decide

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:30.430
that I want to do some kind
of PKI type of environment,

00:19:30.430 --> 00:19:32.414
then that's available
for you, as well.

00:19:32.414 --> 00:19:33.830
And I want to put
that out there's

00:19:33.830 --> 00:19:37.010
a box on the bottom right
corner that this is also

00:19:37.010 --> 00:19:39.740
available for cloud
KMS and software.

00:19:39.740 --> 00:19:41.750
So everything that
I've discussed here

00:19:41.750 --> 00:19:43.280
in terms of asymmetric
keys, you're

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:45.110
free to do this, as
well, in software

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:47.000
if you choose to
just use something

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:52.630
that's more globally distributed
and cheaper and faster.

00:19:52.630 --> 00:19:55.500
Any questions about that?

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:56.380
All right, cool.

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:01.890
Now, statement attestation.

00:20:01.890 --> 00:20:04.820
This is where we provide
you with the confidence

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:09.120
that your keys were actually
created in the HSM itself.

00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:11.850
We could always say that,
yes, please trust us.

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:13.410
But we thought
that you guys might

00:20:13.410 --> 00:20:15.743
feel a little better about
being able to trust something

00:20:15.743 --> 00:20:18.200
more than my word for
it, standing up here

00:20:18.200 --> 00:20:20.670
on this pedestal.

00:20:20.670 --> 00:20:23.040
So what the statement
attestation is

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:26.040
is that it's a cryptographically
signed statement

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:28.070
from the actual HSM hardware.

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:31.020
And we're not using
any kind of Google

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:32.852
developed or branded hardware.

00:20:32.852 --> 00:20:34.560
We're actually using
third-party hardware

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:37.440
that's created by a
third-party manufacturer.

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:39.930
And so the device
itself will provide you

00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:42.000
with a cryptographically
signed statement that

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:45.420
says that this key was actually
created within our hardware

00:20:45.420 --> 00:20:48.630
boundary, within the FIPS
boundary, which is probably

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:49.480
something important.

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:49.980
Yes?

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:52.080
AUDIENCE: Can you share it with
a third-party HSM manufacturer?

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:53.538
IL-SUNG LEE: The
question is can we

00:20:53.538 --> 00:20:55.795
share it with that third
party HSM manufacturers.

00:20:55.795 --> 00:20:57.380
It's Cavium.

00:20:57.380 --> 00:20:59.520
Cavium.

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:00.927
There's no point
in trying to not

00:21:00.927 --> 00:21:03.260
say it because when you
actually look at the attestation

00:21:03.260 --> 00:21:03.759
statement.

00:21:03.759 --> 00:21:05.190
You're going to
see the signature.

00:21:05.190 --> 00:21:09.850
And you're going to see that
it's not a Google signature.

00:21:09.850 --> 00:21:11.425
Now, the signature
can actually be--

00:21:11.425 --> 00:21:12.050
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:21:12.050 --> 00:21:15.160
I'm really sorry, I'm not sure--

00:21:15.160 --> 00:21:17.160
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:20.390
I'll tell you what it is.

00:21:20.390 --> 00:21:22.147
I'm doing demo on
this laptop later

00:21:22.147 --> 00:21:23.730
and they told me
that in order to keep

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:25.380
the screen from
locking that I should

00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:27.175
play YouTube in the background.

00:21:27.175 --> 00:21:29.050
So I have Schubert
running in the background,

00:21:29.050 --> 00:21:30.910
in case you're
wondering what that was.

00:21:30.910 --> 00:21:31.685
OK.

00:21:31.685 --> 00:21:34.219
AUDIENCE: Can we
listen to [INAUDIBLE]??

00:21:34.219 --> 00:21:36.510
IL-SUNG LEE: Well, the talk's
not going that badly yet,

00:21:36.510 --> 00:21:37.500
so we'll see.

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:43.380
So that statement of attestation
could be verified manually.

00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:46.290
The manufacturer will
publish the format

00:21:46.290 --> 00:21:48.317
of what the attestation
statement looks like.

00:21:48.317 --> 00:21:50.400
And we will provide scripts
that you can download.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:51.690
