00:25:43.394 --> 00:25:44.840
OK.

00:25:44.840 --> 00:25:48.604
So what I'm going to show you is
I'm going to show you a script.

00:25:48.604 --> 00:25:50.270
I was going to kind
of walk through this

00:25:50.270 --> 00:25:54.020
rather than see you guys type
on stage and make mistakes,

00:25:54.020 --> 00:25:57.470
I want to show you through
a script how things work,

00:25:57.470 --> 00:25:58.050
essentially.

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:00.860
And I'll walk you through
doing a signing operation.

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:02.910
Plus, I'll show you how
to actually validate

00:26:02.910 --> 00:26:05.860
that attestation.

00:26:05.860 --> 00:26:12.147
So if you look here, so
now what we're going to do

00:26:12.147 --> 00:26:13.480
is that we are going to create--

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:16.880
sorry.

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:20.750
We're going to go
create an EC3A4 key.

00:26:20.750 --> 00:26:23.337
And so you can see
that right here.

00:26:23.337 --> 00:26:24.420
That's one of the options.

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:27.320
And it's got a purpose
of EC signatures.

00:26:27.320 --> 00:26:28.670
And so that's going get created.

00:26:32.002 --> 00:26:33.460
So the next thing
we're going to do

00:26:33.460 --> 00:26:35.460
is we're going to
retrieve the public key.

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:37.955
OK, so a simple thing to do.

00:26:37.955 --> 00:26:39.580
And also another
thing I should mention

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:40.900
is that when we create
the key, we actually

00:26:40.900 --> 00:26:42.430
requested the attestation file.

00:26:42.430 --> 00:26:44.389
That's what the dash
dash attestation file is.

00:26:44.389 --> 00:26:46.180
And then the next thing
is that we actually

00:26:46.180 --> 00:26:47.595
just request a public key.

00:26:47.595 --> 00:26:50.519
And so that's going to
download that, as well.

00:26:50.519 --> 00:26:51.560
And there's a public key.

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:54.375
It's Base64 encoded.

00:26:54.375 --> 00:26:57.540
And the next thing we do is we
just create some dummy data.

00:26:57.540 --> 00:26:59.570
So foo in some file.

00:26:59.570 --> 00:27:01.075
And what we're
going to do now is

00:27:01.075 --> 00:27:04.340
that we're going to go and call
the HSM to actually go and sign

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:06.590
the key.

00:27:06.590 --> 00:27:08.200
I'm sorry, sign the data.

00:27:08.200 --> 00:27:11.060
And so in this case, we're using
the ECKey that we just created

00:27:11.060 --> 00:27:12.320
and passing it the data.

00:27:12.320 --> 00:27:14.610
Now, it's going to come
back with the JSON file.

00:27:14.610 --> 00:27:18.800
And the JSON file provides one
of the fields called signature.

00:27:18.800 --> 00:27:22.820
So this right here is the
Base64 encoded signature.

00:27:22.820 --> 00:27:26.070
Now, my apologies to
the people in the back.

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:28.610
But you don't really need to
see what the actual values are,

00:27:28.610 --> 00:27:29.580
obviously, right?

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:31.970
It's just Base64 encoded
on this signature.

00:27:31.970 --> 00:27:34.310
And so right now the next
thing that I'm going to do

00:27:34.310 --> 00:27:36.101
is that I'm going to
actually extract that,

00:27:36.101 --> 00:27:37.710
put that signature to a file.

00:27:37.710 --> 00:27:39.560
And then I'm going
to use OpenSSL.

00:27:39.560 --> 00:27:42.080
So the OpenSSL is
now running locally.

00:27:42.080 --> 00:27:44.810
And it's going to verify that
that signature corresponds

00:27:44.810 --> 00:27:47.434
to the data that we just signed.

00:27:47.434 --> 00:27:48.850
And hopefully it
says verified OK.

00:27:48.850 --> 00:27:50.180
It says verified OK.

00:27:50.180 --> 00:27:52.340
So the signature
that we generate

00:27:52.340 --> 00:27:56.110
with the HSM, Cloud
HSM, corresponds

00:27:56.110 --> 00:27:59.031
to what OpenSSL just verified.

00:27:59.031 --> 00:28:00.530
Now that we've done
that, let's just

00:28:00.530 --> 00:28:03.740
say that we want to make sure
that we're actually using

00:28:03.740 --> 00:28:05.460
the proper key in the HSM.

00:28:05.460 --> 00:28:08.210
And we want make sure that
it's not some software key.

00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:11.960
So we have that attestation file
that we just download, right?

00:28:11.960 --> 00:28:17.000
And what this Python script that
you can download off the web

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:22.300
does is that it actually
takes this set of certs,

00:28:22.300 --> 00:28:24.020
this cert bundle,
and it actually

00:28:24.020 --> 00:28:26.900
verifies that the signature
associated with the attestation

00:28:26.900 --> 00:28:31.770
statement corresponds with
the certs in the cert bundle.

00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:33.646
Kind of make sense?

00:28:33.646 --> 00:28:35.380
OK.

00:28:35.380 --> 00:28:37.285
And-- oh, sorry.

00:28:41.570 --> 00:28:46.566
Oh, I didn't install the
Python Handler for OpenSSL.

00:28:46.566 --> 00:28:47.690
So we'll skip that for now.

00:28:47.690 --> 00:28:49.540
But essentially what
that comes back with,

00:28:49.540 --> 00:28:51.860
it says it's verified.

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:53.360
And then the next
thing that we have

00:28:53.360 --> 00:28:55.880
is that we can actually query
the different attributes

00:28:55.880 --> 00:28:58.520
within the
attestation statement.

00:28:58.520 --> 00:29:01.130
And so if you look
through-- and I just

00:29:01.130 --> 00:29:03.860
want to point out a few things,
that there's a lot of things

00:29:03.860 --> 00:29:05.201
here.

00:29:05.201 --> 00:29:05.700
Oh, geez.

00:29:05.700 --> 00:29:07.590
I hope you guys can see.

00:29:07.590 --> 00:29:09.090
There you go.

00:29:09.090 --> 00:29:11.010
OK.

00:29:11.010 --> 00:29:13.320
There's a few things I
want to point out here.

00:29:13.320 --> 00:29:17.210
One is that if you look at
this, there's an ID field here.

00:29:17.210 --> 00:29:19.730
This ID field is
basically a concatenation

00:29:19.730 --> 00:29:25.070
of a random string plus the
SHA hash of the actual key ID.

00:29:25.070 --> 00:29:28.160
So you can verify that by
matching the two together.

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:30.230
And actually, the script
will help you do that.

00:29:30.230 --> 00:29:32.180
And then there's a EKCV.

00:29:32.180 --> 00:29:33.200
That's a public key.

00:29:33.200 --> 00:29:36.980
That's a hash, to SHA 256
of the public key itself.

00:29:36.980 --> 00:29:38.960
So you've already
downloaded the public key.

00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:42.081
You can verify the hash against
what the HSM is reporting.

00:29:42.081 --> 00:29:44.330
And this gives you a nice
cryptographically strong way

00:29:44.330 --> 00:29:47.276
of actually validating that
they correspond with each other.

00:29:47.276 --> 00:29:48.900
And this is something
you can obviously

00:29:48.900 --> 00:29:50.674
do only for asymmetric keys.

00:29:50.674 --> 00:29:52.340
One other thing I
want to point out here

00:29:52.340 --> 00:29:54.423
is that if you look in the
private key attestation

00:29:54.423 --> 00:29:58.820
statement section, there is
a file called extractable,

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:01.400
right there, and
it's set to zero.

00:30:01.400 --> 00:30:04.160
And what that's saying is
that the HSM is confirming

00:30:04.160 --> 00:30:06.290
that the key is not exportable.

00:30:06.290 --> 00:30:09.760
So we now have proof
directly from the hardware

00:30:09.760 --> 00:30:11.810
that the key that you're
trying to refer to

00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:14.920
is actually not
extractable, as well.

00:30:14.920 --> 00:30:15.839
Yeah, please?

00:30:15.839 --> 00:30:18.134
AUDIENCE: If we don't get
this attestation statement,

00:30:18.134 --> 00:30:20.890
is it in the log files for HSM?

00:30:20.890 --> 00:30:22.700
IL-SUNG LEE: The actual--

00:30:22.700 --> 00:30:23.340
which one?

00:30:23.340 --> 00:30:24.060
The attestation statement?

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:25.860
Because you can download the
attestation statement any time

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:26.463
you like.

00:30:26.463 --> 00:30:27.004
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:30:27.004 --> 00:30:27.760
IL-SUNG LEE: Yeah.

00:30:27.760 --> 00:30:31.110
You don't have to do it
right at key creation.

00:30:31.110 --> 00:30:32.670
The other thing is
that you can also

00:30:32.670 --> 00:30:34.350
use this to actually
help validate

00:30:34.350 --> 00:30:38.100
that the resource name
matches what was reported

00:30:38.100 --> 00:30:39.570
in the attestation statement.

00:30:39.570 --> 00:30:43.460
And the Python script
here helps you do this.

00:30:43.460 --> 00:30:46.884
And so it verified here that
the keys match in attestation.

00:30:46.884 --> 00:30:48.300
And then another
thing it'll do is

00:30:48.300 --> 00:30:50.940
that it'll match that the public
key that you've downloaded

00:30:50.940 --> 00:30:55.260
matches the hash that's in the
attestation statement, as well.

00:30:55.260 --> 00:30:57.030
And so you can do that, as well.

00:30:57.030 --> 00:31:01.110
And it comes back and
says that it matched.

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:04.560
So this is a really, really
a cool way of actually--

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:07.020
you know, especially if
you're a crypto nerd--

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:09.329
to be very secure
and really, really

00:31:09.329 --> 00:31:11.370
feel comfortable with the
fact that I am actually

00:31:11.370 --> 00:31:13.590
talking to the hardware device.

00:31:13.590 --> 00:31:18.290
I'm talking-- I'm using a
key that I think I'm using.

