00:32:58.370 --> 00:32:59.370
you initially create it.

00:32:59.370 --> 00:33:09.999
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:09.999 --> 00:33:10.790
IL-SUNG LEE: Right.

00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:13.017
So the comment is
that you can usually

00:33:13.017 --> 00:33:15.110
use the same key for,
for example, email

00:33:15.110 --> 00:33:17.360
for doing both signing
and encryption.

00:33:17.360 --> 00:33:19.167
That is true.

00:33:19.167 --> 00:33:20.750
The way that we've
designed this right

00:33:20.750 --> 00:33:23.974
now is that we want to make
an explicit intention around

00:33:23.974 --> 00:33:26.390
to know what the keys are used
for so that we can actually

00:33:26.390 --> 00:33:28.785
limit the scope of
what the keys can do.

00:33:28.785 --> 00:33:31.160
If there's a reason that you
think that we should be able

00:33:31.160 --> 00:33:32.934
support both with
the same key, then

00:33:32.934 --> 00:33:34.100
I'm very happy to hear this.

00:33:34.100 --> 00:33:36.354
And we can talk about
this in the future.

00:33:36.354 --> 00:33:37.230
Yeah.

00:33:37.230 --> 00:33:38.580
Thank you.

00:33:38.580 --> 00:33:40.088
Yeah, please.

00:33:40.088 --> 00:33:45.720
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:45.720 --> 00:33:49.380
IL-SUNG LEE: So the question is
whether the asymmetric software

00:33:49.380 --> 00:33:51.420
keys or GA or--

00:33:51.420 --> 00:33:54.450
I mean, sorry, if they're
out or what's the status.

00:33:54.450 --> 00:33:57.240
Currently right now they
are in the same situation

00:33:57.240 --> 00:33:58.110
as Cloud HSM.

00:33:58.110 --> 00:34:00.220
They're in alpha.

00:34:00.220 --> 00:34:03.075
And so when Cloud HSM
becomes available in beta

00:34:03.075 --> 00:34:05.250
and becomes public,
you can expect

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:09.315
to see the asymmetric keys being
available in software, as well.

00:34:09.315 --> 00:34:11.175
OK.

00:34:11.175 --> 00:34:13.642
All right.

00:34:13.642 --> 00:34:19.065
So that was a demo, actually,
for just showing you

00:34:19.065 --> 00:34:21.780
the attestations and making
sure that we can actually

00:34:21.780 --> 00:34:23.155
prove this.

00:34:23.155 --> 00:34:24.780
Is there any questions
around the demo?

00:34:27.929 --> 00:34:28.822
All right.

00:34:28.822 --> 00:34:30.530
Can we switch back to
the slides, please?

00:34:30.530 --> 00:34:31.520
Thank you.

00:34:31.520 --> 00:34:32.970
Yes, please.

00:34:32.970 --> 00:34:37.388
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:37.388 --> 00:34:38.929
IL-SUNG LEE: I'm
sorry, is the tool--

00:34:38.929 --> 00:34:39.970
AUDIENCE: A paid service.

00:34:39.970 --> 00:34:42.429
IL-SUNG LEE: Oh is
it a paid service?

00:34:42.429 --> 00:34:43.940
Do you mean the Python scripts?

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:44.874
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:34:44.874 --> 00:34:46.290
IL-SUNG LEE: So
the Python scripts

00:34:46.290 --> 00:34:48.145
that we use to
actually verify it,

00:34:48.145 --> 00:34:49.520
it's not going to
be a paid tool.

00:34:49.520 --> 00:34:51.061
It's going to be
something that we're

00:34:51.061 --> 00:34:52.984
going to allow you
to freely download.

00:34:52.984 --> 00:34:54.400
And it will be
something that will

00:34:54.400 --> 00:34:56.066
be published on the
web where there will

00:34:56.066 --> 00:34:57.909
be a link for you download.

00:34:57.909 --> 00:35:04.254
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:04.254 --> 00:35:05.670
IL-SUNG LEE: Yeah,
so the question

00:35:05.670 --> 00:35:08.820
is whether the actual process of
generating a key and verifying

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:10.280
is a paid service.

00:35:10.280 --> 00:35:10.860
Yes.

00:35:10.860 --> 00:35:13.230
So Cloud HSM will
be a paid services,

00:35:13.230 --> 00:35:14.700
just like Cloud KMS is.

00:35:14.700 --> 00:35:20.070
So you will be charged to
do the signing operation.

00:35:20.070 --> 00:35:23.075
But the verification service
operation that I showed you

00:35:23.075 --> 00:35:27.690
was actually done on local
client using OpenSSL.

00:35:27.690 --> 00:35:31.450
So that's something that
you would do on your side.

00:35:31.450 --> 00:35:34.430
We've not yet heard anyone who
had a valid reason for doing

00:35:34.430 --> 00:35:37.270
verification on the HSM itself.

00:35:37.270 --> 00:35:39.900
But it didn't quite
make sense to us.

00:35:39.900 --> 00:35:41.570
But if there is,
I'd love to hear it

00:35:41.570 --> 00:35:44.780
because it eluded us so far.

00:35:44.780 --> 00:35:46.772
AUDIENCE: Not even in
FedRAMP environments?

00:35:46.772 --> 00:35:48.980
IL-SUNG LEE: Not even in
FedRAMP environments, that's

00:35:48.980 --> 00:35:52.340
true, because typically you
don't have those signature

00:35:52.340 --> 00:35:56.105
verifications happen outside--
the signing operation

00:35:56.105 --> 00:35:58.020
is the thing that's
really, really important.

00:35:58.020 --> 00:35:58.724
Yes.

00:35:58.724 --> 00:36:00.600
AUDIENCE: So where
do you kind of

00:36:00.600 --> 00:36:02.516
envision this product,
both for personal users

00:36:02.516 --> 00:36:04.904
and for enterprise users?

00:36:04.904 --> 00:36:07.070
IL-SUNG LEE: The question
is whether I envision this

00:36:07.070 --> 00:36:08.750
for enterprise and personal--

00:36:08.750 --> 00:36:09.696
AUDIENCE: But where?

00:36:09.696 --> 00:36:12.061
Like, where do you kind
of see a personal user

00:36:12.061 --> 00:36:14.872
might use Google HSM?

00:36:14.872 --> 00:36:15.830
IL-SUNG LEE: Oh, I see.

00:36:15.830 --> 00:36:18.110
So the question
is, where I would

00:36:18.110 --> 00:36:21.867
envision someone who uses this
for personal use, perhaps?

00:36:21.867 --> 00:36:23.450
You know, I've got
to be quite honest,

00:36:23.450 --> 00:36:24.980
I haven't heard
of a lot of people

00:36:24.980 --> 00:36:29.990
who really feel like using
HSMs for personal use.

00:36:29.990 --> 00:36:32.600
There's typically not anyone
who has a personal reason

00:36:32.600 --> 00:36:35.220
to have something that's
very, very secure like this.

00:36:35.220 --> 00:36:38.210
Now, that being said,
this is a managed API.

00:36:38.210 --> 00:36:39.950
It's on a per usage basis.

00:36:39.950 --> 00:36:42.560
So if all you're doing
is creating a single key

00:36:42.560 --> 00:36:44.690
and doing a few
operations, it's not

00:36:44.690 --> 00:36:46.460
going to cost very much at all.

00:36:46.460 --> 00:36:48.930
And so you're perfectly
welcome to do that.

00:36:48.930 --> 00:36:50.690
But I've only met
one person I know

00:36:50.690 --> 00:36:52.710
that plays with HSMs for fun.

00:36:52.710 --> 00:36:53.980
It's one of our PMs.

00:36:53.980 --> 00:36:55.400
He works out of Kirkland.

00:36:55.400 --> 00:36:56.960
And he likes to
buy them off eBay

00:36:56.960 --> 00:36:58.940
and has a whole
garage full of them.

00:36:58.940 --> 00:37:00.440
And I don't know.

00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:02.523
I don't think there's
something right in his head.

00:37:02.523 --> 00:37:04.190
But I think that he--

00:37:04.190 --> 00:37:07.350
because no one does
that for fun, right?

00:37:07.350 --> 00:37:13.370
But you know, I envision that
this will be very popular

00:37:13.370 --> 00:37:17.080
with a lot of enterprise users.

00:37:17.080 --> 00:37:18.900
For personal users,
I can see it if you

00:37:18.900 --> 00:37:20.650
need to do something
like you want to host

00:37:20.650 --> 00:37:22.309
your certs, for example.

00:37:22.309 --> 00:37:23.600
And you need to actually just--

00:37:23.600 --> 00:37:25.460
you just want to make
sure that's in something

00:37:25.460 --> 00:37:27.751
you feel a little bit more
secure about because it's up

00:37:27.751 --> 00:37:28.430
in the cloud.

00:37:28.430 --> 00:37:31.100
But it's totally up to you.

00:37:31.100 --> 00:37:31.954
Yes?

00:37:31.954 --> 00:37:35.342
AUDIENCE: Will this be available
for Marketplace integrations?

00:37:35.342 --> 00:37:36.340
[INAUDIBLE]

00:37:36.340 --> 00:37:37.090
IL-SUNG LEE: Yeah.

00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:38.465
The question is
whether this will

00:37:38.465 --> 00:37:41.120
be available for
Marketplace integrations.

00:37:41.120 --> 00:37:42.170
No.

00:37:42.170 --> 00:37:45.230
Not the way that
it is right now.

00:37:45.230 --> 00:37:49.750
But anything's
possible in the future.

00:37:49.750 --> 00:37:50.784
Yes, question?

00:37:50.784 --> 00:37:54.576
AUDIENCE: Will it be available
for separate instance of HSM

00:37:54.576 --> 00:37:56.910
or will it be a
multi-tenant [INAUDIBLE]

00:37:56.910 --> 00:37:59.410
IL-SUNG LEE: Oh, the question
is whether this will always be

00:37:59.410 --> 00:38:01.090
multi-tenant, or it can be--

00:38:01.090 --> 00:38:02.500
yeah.

00:38:02.500 --> 00:38:04.600
Right now, this is
envisioned to be

00:38:04.600 --> 00:38:07.820
purely a multi-tenant service.

00:38:07.820 --> 00:38:09.250
The reason is that
in order for us

00:38:09.250 --> 00:38:12.310
to provide the amount of scale
and flexibility that we have

00:38:12.310 --> 00:38:14.860
and kind of design it
to be basically exposed

00:38:14.860 --> 00:38:17.040
as an API service, it
was necessary to make

00:38:17.040 --> 00:38:17.800
a multi-tenant.

00:38:17.800 --> 00:38:20.590
And also economies of scale,
and all the other great reasons

00:38:20.590 --> 00:38:22.060
people use the cloud.

00:38:22.060 --> 00:38:24.420
But yeah, that's
a great question.

00:38:24.420 --> 00:38:26.926
It's multi-tenant.

00:38:26.926 --> 00:38:29.860
Yes.

00:38:29.860 --> 00:38:40.180
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:38:40.180 --> 00:38:40.930
IL-SUNG LEE: Yeah.

00:38:40.930 --> 00:38:43.430
So the first question
is, will the HSMs

00:38:43.430 --> 00:38:45.770
be available in all regions?

00:38:45.770 --> 00:38:49.950
The HSMs will be
available globally.

00:38:49.950 --> 00:38:53.190
And it may or may not be
available in all regions.

00:38:53.190 --> 00:38:54.840
And so as I said
before, it's not

00:38:54.840 --> 00:38:58.110
as easy as deploying code
and pushing out software

00:38:58.110 --> 00:39:00.120
because there's a lot
more process involved

00:39:00.120 --> 00:39:02.670
in trying to get devices
put into the data center.

00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:05.220
But we will make sure that's
represented in the places

00:39:05.220 --> 00:39:07.540
where it's most
valuable to people.

00:39:07.540 --> 00:39:09.630
And so if there's
a particular area

00:39:09.630 --> 00:39:11.280
that you guys care
about a lot, then I

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:13.238
encourage you to come
and talk to me afterwards

00:39:13.238 --> 00:39:14.699
and we can chat more about this.

00:39:14.699 --> 00:39:16.740
And then the second question
was whether this can

00:39:16.740 --> 00:39:19.850
be integrated HashiCorp Vault.

00:39:19.850 --> 00:39:21.000
It already can.

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:23.130
It already is because
essentially, HashiCorp Vault

00:39:23.130 --> 00:39:25.514
is already integrated
with our Cloud KMS.

00:39:25.514 --> 00:39:27.180
And as you just saw,
the only difference

00:39:27.180 --> 00:39:30.570
between Cloud KMS and Cloud
HSM is a flag when you actually

00:39:30.570 --> 00:39:31.650
create the key.

00:39:31.650 --> 00:39:33.750
So to HashiCorp Vault,
it looks like just

00:39:33.750 --> 00:39:37.240
like a regular KSM key.

00:39:37.240 --> 00:39:38.640
All right.

00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:41.020
So the final thing
I want to talk about

00:39:41.020 --> 00:39:44.890
is the CMEK integration, or
Customer Managed Encryption

00:39:44.890 --> 00:39:45.850
Keys.

00:39:45.850 --> 00:39:48.430
So this, I think, is a
really, really great feature

00:39:48.430 --> 00:39:52.390
because a lot of people
put data into the cloud.

00:39:52.390 --> 00:39:54.880
And they want to make sure
that they have some control

00:39:54.880 --> 00:39:56.770
over those cloud services.

00:39:56.770 --> 00:40:00.130
And right now what you can
do is that you can actually

00:40:00.130 --> 00:40:03.140
put data in BigQuery, for
example, which is already GA.

00:40:03.140 --> 00:40:06.000
And you will be able
to actually associate

00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:08.080
the encryption key
that ultimately

00:40:08.080 --> 00:40:10.780
protects the data in Cloud KMS.

00:40:10.780 --> 00:40:12.915
And consequently,
since Cloud KMS

00:40:12.915 --> 00:40:16.720
is integrated with Cloud
HSM, in a Cloud HSM key.

00:40:16.720 --> 00:40:18.850
And it's available not
only for BigQuery, but also

00:40:18.850 --> 00:40:21.100
for Compute Engine,
which is in beta, also

00:40:21.100 --> 00:40:24.940
for Cloud Storage and Dataproc.

00:40:24.940 --> 00:40:26.500
And so what I'd
like to do now is

00:40:26.500 --> 00:40:29.980
I'd like to actually show you
a quick demo of how this works

00:40:29.980 --> 00:40:30.890
and how simple it is.

00:40:30.890 --> 00:40:33.730
And so if you've already used
Customer Managed Encryption

00:40:33.730 --> 00:40:36.040
Keys already, then this
is probably something

00:40:36.040 --> 00:40:37.959
that you've already
gone through.

00:40:37.959 --> 00:40:40.000
But I think it's kind of
neat to actually realize

00:40:40.000 --> 00:40:41.958
that everything is being
protected, ultimately,

00:40:41.958 --> 00:40:43.240
by a key in the HSM.

00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:46.020
If you can switch over, please.

00:40:46.020 --> 00:40:46.830
OK.

00:40:46.830 --> 00:40:49.820
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:40:49.820 --> 00:40:52.790
going to first start off
back in the KMS console.

00:40:52.790 --> 00:40:55.490
And remember that when we've
actually used Customer Managed

00:40:55.490 --> 00:40:58.230
Encryption Keys, we're
talking about symmetric keys.

00:40:58.230 --> 00:41:02.450
So let's go and create
a symmetric key here.

00:41:02.450 --> 00:41:05.580
Let's just call it HSM demo--

00:41:05.580 --> 00:41:09.659
whoops-- demo sym key.

00:41:09.659 --> 00:41:11.200
And it's going to
be a symmetric key.

00:41:11.200 --> 00:41:13.270
And we'll put it in
hardware because that's

00:41:13.270 --> 00:41:15.350
what you guys are
all here to look at.

00:41:15.350 --> 00:41:18.050
And I'm going to create it.

00:41:18.050 --> 00:41:22.130
So HSM-- I apologize
for all the keys but--

00:41:22.130 --> 00:41:25.070
so here's a key that
we just created.

00:41:25.070 --> 00:41:28.337
And I'm just going to go
through and I'm going to--

00:41:28.337 --> 00:41:30.920
well, I guess I don't need to
do this, but I'll do it anyways.

00:41:30.920 --> 00:41:32.461
So what I'll do is
that I will now go

00:41:32.461 --> 00:41:36.760
to create a new bucket in GCS.

00:41:36.760 --> 00:41:40.960
And I'll just say that
this is an HSM demo--

00:41:40.960 --> 00:41:44.165
bucket-- whoops, bucket two.

00:41:44.165 --> 00:41:46.660
Because I might have
already created bucket one.

00:41:46.660 --> 00:41:50.100
Require regional, US Central.

00:41:50.100 --> 00:41:51.450
And here's the thing.

00:41:51.450 --> 00:41:56.220
In advanced settings, you can
actually go right from the UI

00:41:56.220 --> 00:41:59.230
and choose to say that I want
this to be managed by Google,

00:41:59.230 --> 00:42:00.855
which is the default
encryption, or you

00:42:00.855 --> 00:42:03.521
can choose to say that I want to
use customer managed encryption

00:42:03.521 --> 00:42:04.060
keys.

00:42:04.060 --> 00:42:06.630
And remember, it doesn't
care whether it's

00:42:06.630 --> 00:42:08.850
a KMS key or an HSM key.

00:42:08.850 --> 00:42:11.360
And so all you have to do is
say, customer managed key.

00:42:11.360 --> 00:42:13.020
I'm going to select the key.

00:42:13.020 --> 00:42:17.000
And just to be faster, I'm going
to type in the actual key name.

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:17.942
I do that.

00:42:17.942 --> 00:42:19.650
And it's nice enough
to actually tell you

00:42:19.650 --> 00:42:23.310
that in order for this to work,
the service account associated

00:42:23.310 --> 00:42:28.330
with your instance of GCS
needs access to that key.

00:42:28.330 --> 00:42:29.950
And it will actually
do that for you.

00:42:29.950 --> 00:42:31.033
So I'm going to hit Grant.

00:42:34.970 --> 00:42:39.790
And so now I have
created this bucket.

00:42:39.790 --> 00:42:41.320
Whoops.

00:42:41.320 --> 00:42:44.720
Did I miss-- oh,
I've already got two.

00:42:44.720 --> 00:42:46.050
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:42:46.050 --> 00:42:47.300
IL-SUNG LEE: Oh, you're right.

00:42:47.300 --> 00:42:47.850
You're right.

00:42:47.850 --> 00:42:49.530
This always bugs me about this.

00:42:49.530 --> 00:42:51.469
I should go and talk to them.

00:42:51.469 --> 00:42:52.010
There you go.

00:42:54.980 --> 00:42:56.352
Yeah.

00:42:56.352 --> 00:42:57.810
So I'm just going
to upload a file.

00:42:57.810 --> 00:43:00.200
It's going to be a stupid
file, like sample text.

00:43:00.200 --> 00:43:03.330
I think that if I
recall, this file,

00:43:03.330 --> 00:43:06.980
all it says is
that this is data.

00:43:06.980 --> 00:43:09.619
Let's see.

00:43:09.619 --> 00:43:10.160
There you go.

00:43:10.160 --> 00:43:11.409
Test data for bucket.

00:43:11.409 --> 00:43:13.075
So now what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:43:13.075 --> 00:43:15.080
to actually go to KMS now.

00:43:15.080 --> 00:43:19.175
And this key that we just
created, this HSM demo sym key,

00:43:19.175 --> 00:43:20.420
I'm going to go there.

00:43:20.420 --> 00:43:22.360
And you can see that--

00:43:22.360 --> 00:43:22.970
oops.

00:43:22.970 --> 00:43:26.220
I'm going to go
to the key itself.

00:43:26.220 --> 00:43:30.230
And you can see on the side here
that it lists the permissions.

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:32.000
And sure enough,
you can see the fact

00:43:32.000 --> 00:43:35.300
that the service account for
GCS was already added there.

00:43:35.300 --> 00:43:36.721
That was done automatically.

00:43:36.721 --> 00:43:38.346
So I'm just going to
copy this, just so

00:43:38.346 --> 00:43:40.130
that I can add it later.

00:43:40.130 --> 00:43:42.850
And I'm going to delete it.

00:43:42.850 --> 00:43:46.010
And so I have now effectively
deleted this access.

00:43:46.010 --> 00:43:47.880
So what happens now?

00:43:47.880 --> 00:43:50.065
So what happens is that
now when I go back to GCS,

00:43:50.065 --> 00:43:52.160
and I try to go and
read any of the the data

00:43:52.160 --> 00:43:54.806
on that bucket,
first of all, GCS

00:43:54.806 --> 00:43:56.430
does its own permission
check and says,

00:43:56.430 --> 00:43:57.430
yeah, you guys are good.

00:43:57.430 --> 00:43:59.840
Now let me figure out
which data encryption keys

00:43:59.840 --> 00:44:01.040
I need to decrypt.

00:44:01.040 --> 00:44:03.200
And it's going to
send that over to KMS.

00:44:03.200 --> 00:44:05.450
And then KMS is going
to go and see those keys

00:44:05.450 --> 00:44:08.750
and say that, OK, let me see if
you're allowed to decrypt this.

00:44:08.750 --> 00:44:11.172
And it's going to see that
you're not allowed to do this.

00:44:11.172 --> 00:44:12.880
And it's going to give
you access denied.

00:44:12.880 --> 00:44:14.930
Or at least that's what
we hope will happen.

00:44:14.930 --> 00:44:18.680
It usually takes around a
minute or so for the changes

00:44:18.680 --> 00:44:19.931
to propagate.

00:44:19.931 --> 00:44:21.930
So you might have to try
this a couple of times.

00:44:21.930 --> 00:44:25.310
So let's see if it
works right now.

00:44:25.310 --> 00:44:26.480
Yeah, it works.

00:44:26.480 --> 00:44:28.505
So we'll give it a
few more seconds.

00:44:28.505 --> 00:44:29.546
AUDIENCE: Quick question.

00:44:29.546 --> 00:44:32.036
So you just created a DEK.

00:44:32.036 --> 00:44:36.520
Is there a KEK-DEK model
for this [INAUDIBLE]??

00:44:36.520 --> 00:44:38.830
IL-SUNG LEE: So the question
was, I just created a DEK.

00:44:38.830 --> 00:44:40.189
Is there a DEK-KEK model.

00:44:40.189 --> 00:44:41.480
I actually didn't create a DEK.

00:44:41.480 --> 00:44:42.920
I actually created a KEK.

00:44:42.920 --> 00:44:44.420
So it's a data encryption key.

00:44:44.420 --> 00:44:47.332
It's keys that actually
encrypt the base data.

00:44:47.332 --> 00:44:49.040
And then above that,
the second level key

00:44:49.040 --> 00:44:50.402
is a key encryption key.

00:44:50.402 --> 00:44:52.610
When you talk about customer
managed encryption keys,

00:44:52.610 --> 00:44:55.220
you're always talking about
that second-level key encryption

00:44:55.220 --> 00:44:56.690
key.

00:44:56.690 --> 00:45:00.110
And so what happens is that in
a normal case, in a GCS bucket,

00:45:00.110 --> 00:45:03.230
you have the bucket
encrypted with local keys.

00:45:03.230 --> 00:45:05.715
And then it goes up
to the internal KMS.

00:45:05.715 --> 00:45:09.810
In the CMEK model, that
goes to Cloud KMS, instead.

00:45:09.810 --> 00:45:11.630
So when you need to
decrypt those keys,

00:45:11.630 --> 00:45:14.250
it actually goes and
contacts Cloud KMS and says,

00:45:14.250 --> 00:45:16.850
can you please use
this specified key

00:45:16.850 --> 00:45:19.462
to encrypt my data
encryption keys.

00:45:19.462 --> 00:45:21.970
AUDIENCE: So you just link
the KEK and then [INAUDIBLE]

00:45:21.970 --> 00:45:23.420
IL-SUNG LEE: Correct.

00:45:23.420 --> 00:45:26.680
So now if I do this, I
think it's enough time,

00:45:26.680 --> 00:45:28.970
you get the permission
denied on Cloud KMS key.

00:45:28.970 --> 00:45:29.780
It's really small.

00:45:29.780 --> 00:45:30.080
I'm sorry.

00:45:30.080 --> 00:45:31.455
But that's what
it says up there.

00:45:31.455 --> 00:45:35.690
And people in the front
can attest to it, as well.

00:45:35.690 --> 00:45:39.262
Now basically I just proved
that it works the way it does.

00:45:39.262 --> 00:45:40.970
You now can you use
your keys to actually

00:45:40.970 --> 00:45:43.650
have some granularity
around control of your data.

00:45:43.650 --> 00:45:46.430
So now if you go
back to Cloud KMS

00:45:46.430 --> 00:45:49.885
and decide that we're going
to re-add the permission,

00:45:49.885 --> 00:45:55.460
and we'll give it back the
encrypter decrypter role,

00:45:55.460 --> 00:46:00.240
add that, then we
expect to see that this

00:46:00.240 --> 00:46:03.080
is going to work again.

00:46:03.080 --> 00:46:04.670
And sure enough, it works again.

00:46:04.670 --> 00:46:06.520
So it's as simple as that.

00:46:06.520 --> 00:46:08.380
So this is not
really anything new

00:46:08.380 --> 00:46:10.630
if you've been using customer
managed encryption keys.

00:46:10.630 --> 00:46:12.296
But it's just a quick
way of showing you

00:46:12.296 --> 00:46:14.170
that really nothing changes.

00:46:14.170 --> 00:46:17.550
You've just been able to use an
HSM key to secure your data out

00:46:17.550 --> 00:46:19.030
in GCS.

00:46:19.030 --> 00:46:20.920
And I'm looking at
the time right now,

00:46:20.920 --> 00:46:22.180
so we're running out--

00:46:22.180 --> 00:46:24.670
I was going to do the
same thing with BigQuery,

00:46:24.670 --> 00:46:26.960
but it's essentially
the same thing, as well.

00:46:26.960 --> 00:46:28.960
You can actually just use
BigQuery, associate it

00:46:28.960 --> 00:46:30.820
with the HSM key,
and then remove

00:46:30.820 --> 00:46:33.510
permissions and all of a
sudden, things start to break.

00:46:33.510 --> 00:46:34.780
OK.

00:46:34.780 --> 00:46:36.030
So going back to the slides.

00:46:39.720 --> 00:46:43.270
Now to summarize,
what we learned today

00:46:43.270 --> 00:46:46.420
is that you can actually
use some of the things

00:46:46.420 --> 00:46:49.020
that we provided now to
help meet your compliance

00:46:49.020 --> 00:46:49.960
objectives.

00:46:49.960 --> 00:46:51.950
Previously before, if
you came to us and said,

00:46:51.950 --> 00:46:54.160
do you have anything
that can help me satisfy

00:46:54.160 --> 00:46:57.130
the requirements for a
FIPS 104-2 level three

00:46:57.130 --> 00:47:00.880
device or three crypto manager,
then we didn't have an answer.

00:47:00.880 --> 00:47:03.920
But we now can actually tell
you, please use Cloud HSM.

00:47:03.920 --> 00:47:07.337
The devices are
all FIPS validated.

00:47:07.337 --> 00:47:09.670
You can meet your security
requirements around the keys.

00:47:09.670 --> 00:47:11.180
If you have more
strict requirements

00:47:11.180 --> 00:47:12.790
